# Stationer & Dry Goods, Business District.
# Mr. Finch: local merchant, sells ledgers, twine, and wax; observer of town habits.
NPC: stationer
LOCATION: business_district
SCHEDULE: morning=stationer, midday=stationer, evening=stationer, night=closed

TOPIC: default
  GATE: NOT topic_done(stationer, default) AND coat = plain
  TAG: stationer_plain
  MR. FINCH: "Two-cent ruled slates on the counter, linen correspondence paper in the drawers."
  [He wraps a cake of red sealing wax in brown paper and ties it with a short length of hemp twine.]
  MR. FINCH: "You have ink on your knuckles, friend. Same as any man who spends his evenings arguing with ledgers."

TOPIC: default
  GATE: NOT topic_done(stationer, default)
  TAG: stationer_badge
  MR. FINCH: "Officer Corwin. If this is the quarterly precinct supply voucher, Odell hasn't endorsed it."
  WALTER CORWIN: "I'm not here for blotters, Mr. Finch."
  MR. FINCH: "Then I hope you're here as a paying customer.\nA shop runs on sales, Officer, not on town curiosity."

TOPIC: the_corkboard_twine
  GATE: always
  TAG: stationer_twine
  LABEL: "Ask about heavy wrapping twine and map pins"
  TIME: 5
  WALTER CORWIN: "I need another spool of the tarred hemp twine. And brass-headed pins."
  MR. FINCH: "Working late in your room above the cobbler again, are you?"
  [He sets a wooden bobbin of dark twine on the counter.]
  MR. FINCH: "Most men who buy that twine use it to hang dried cod in the shed.\nYou're the only man in this county who strings it between index cards."
  WALTER CORWIN: "It keeps the facts from drifting."
  MR. FINCH: "Facts don't drift, Walter. Men do.\nWhen my ledger shows an inventory deficit, I don't string twine across the shelves. I look at who holds the key."
  NOTEBOOK: stationer_twine_1 | "Mr. Finch: 'Facts don't drift, Walter. Men do.' Observed that only Walter uses merchant twine to bind cards."

TOPIC: fenn_orders
  GATE: evidence(knife)
  TAG: stationer_parchment
  LABEL: "Ask about Dr. Fenn's stationery orders"
  TIME: 4
  WALTER CORWIN: "Dr. Aldous Fenn bought his calfskin notebooks here."
  MR. FINCH: "Heavy imported vellum. Octavo size, marbled endpapers.\nHe insisted on iron-gall ink. Said modern aniline dyes fade inside thirty years, and he intended his observations to outlast the century."
  WALTER CORWIN: "What was he writing?"
  MR. FINCH: "He didn't share his notes with a tradesman, Officer.\nThough he did buy two dozen wax candles every autumn. The thick spermaceti ones that burn without smoke in closed cellars."
  NOTEBOOK: stationer_fenn_supplies | "Dr. Fenn purchased iron-gall ink and heavy smokeless spermaceti candles for closed-cellar work."
  EVIDENCE: cellar_candles

TOPIC: naomi_supplies
  GATE: evidence(naomi) AND NOT topic_done(stationer, naomi_supplies)
  TAG: stationer_naomi
  LABEL: "Ask if Naomi Freeman purchased writing materials"
  TIME: 5
  WALTER CORWIN: "Did Naomi Freeman come into the shop before she died?"
  MR. FINCH: "The colored woman staying at Mrs. Almy's? She came in on a Thursday."
  WALTER CORWIN: "What did she buy?"
  MR. FINCH: "A one-cent graphite pencil. And two sheets of heavy draughting linen.\nShe asked if linen would rot if kept in oilskin near salt water. I told her linen outlasts paper three to one in the damp."
  [He pauses, resting both palms on the polished counter.]
  MR. FINCH: "She wasn't writing a letter, Walter. She was copying a diagram.\nHer hands were steady. Steadier than mine."
  NOTEBOOK: stationer_naomi_linen | "Naomi Freeman bought drafting linen tested against salt-water damp to copy a diagram, using a graphite pencil."
  EVIDENCE: drafting_linen_diagram
